Fixes #97055 (residual cache-identity gap)

AI-assisted: this PR was prepared with Codex and reviewed with Claude.

## What Problem This Solves

The core misrouting from #97055 — custom Ollama memory providers (`ollama-cpu`, `ollama-gpu`, …) being collapsed onto the default `ollama` provider — has already landed on `main` via fa5f24b021e (adapter-side provider-id resolution) and b085184d085 (gateway-side provider-id propagation). This PR has been slimmed down to the remaining gap: the Ollama memory adapter's embedding cache identity is still only `{provider, model, outputDimensionality}`. Two configurations that reuse the same provider id but point at different endpoints (different `baseUrl`, or different non-secret custom headers selecting a different tenant/backend) hash to the same memory provider key and silently reuse stale cached embeddings from the other endpoint.

## Why This Change Was Made

The lmstudio memory adapter already keys its cache by `{provider, baseUrl, model, headers: sanitizeEmbeddingCacheHeaders(...)}`; the Ollama adapter now mirrors that pattern exactly (keeping its existing `outputDimensionality` field), so endpoint identity participates in the cache key consistently across local-server adapters.

**Secret-header hardening:** because `cacheKeyData` is hashed into the memory provider key, a credential header in it would force a full reindex on every credential rotation. `sanitizeEmbeddingCacheHeaders` in `memory-host-sdk` now always drops sensitive header names (`authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, and anything containing `api-key`/`token`/`secret`) for every adapter, and `excludedHeaderNames` becomes optional. Secret headers are still sent on the wire — only the cache identity excludes them.

## User Impact

- Users running multiple Ollama endpoints under distinct provider ids (or re-pointing one id at a new `baseUrl`) get correct, endpoint-scoped embedding caches instead of silent stale reuse.
- Rotating a credential header no longer churns the memory index, for any embedding adapter.
- **Compatibility note (maintainer acceptance item):** adding `baseUrl`/`headers` to the Ollama cache identity changes the provider-key hash for existing Ollama memory indexes (including the default `ollama` provider), so they rebuild once via the normal memory index rebuild flow on upgrade. This is intentional — it is required to distinguish endpoints, and memory indexes are rebuildable.

## Evidence

### Real-behavior proof — the exact provider cache key memory-core persists

This drives the **real** `ollamaMemoryEmbeddingProviderAdapter.create()` for ONE provider id (`ollama`) against two different endpoints, then computes the exact key memory-core writes as the index provider identity: `hashText(JSON.stringify(cacheKeyData))` (`extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-reindex-state.ts:55`). No mocks — real adapter, real hash.

**Before — `origin/main` @ `e43bd3d57bc`** (both endpoints collide on one key → the second endpoint silently reuses the first's cached embeddings):
```
======== REAL ollama memory embedding cache identity ========
provider id (both): ollama    model (both): nomic-embed-text

endpoint A  baseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","model":"nomic-embed-text"}
  providerKey  = 0eeec5c660cc1b0f3d2b83e8f758d4c80fba365db6ec183b4679534368c78085

endpoint B  baseUrl=http://10.0.0.5:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","model":"nomic-embed-text"}
  providerKey  = 0eeec5c660cc1b0f3d2b83e8f758d4c80fba365db6ec183b4679534368c78085

A vs B providerKey differ:            false
```

**After — this branch @ `850ea819308`** (each endpoint gets its own key → a change reindexes; the secret `authorization` header is stripped from the identity so rotating it does not churn the index):
```
======== REAL ollama memory embedding cache identity ========
provider id (both): ollama    model (both): nomic-embed-text

endpoint A  baseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","baseUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:11434","model":"nomic-embed-text","headers":[["Content-Type","application/json"]]}
  providerKey  = 780c71a6cdf63a0d574b4baf77afbf734b7bf3abf3253e177a9654a0658eb7a1

endpoint B  baseUrl=http://10.0.0.5:11434
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","baseUrl":"http://10.0.0.5:11434","model":"nomic-embed-text","headers":[["Content-Type","application/json"]]}
  providerKey  = cd5a7580e8a73582a364a1100623226284f32e4bb8a409fae29f6887ab9919cb

endpoint A + secret authorization header
  cacheKeyData = {"provider":"ollama","baseUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:11434","model":"nomic-embed-text","headers":[["Content-Type","application/json"]]}
  providerKey  = 780c71a6cdf63a0d574b4baf77afbf734b7bf3abf3253e177a9654a0658eb7a1

A vs B providerKey differ:            true
secret token absent from cache data:  true
```

The harness asserts the two endpoints' persisted keys differ and that no secret token appears in the cache identity; it passes on this branch and fails on main (`AssertionError: expected '0eeec5c6…' not to be '0eeec5c6…'`).

<details><summary>Reproduce (drop into <code>extensions/ollama/src/</code> and <code>pnpm test</code> it on either checkout)</summary>

```ts
// Real-behavior proof for issue #97055 / PR #97059 (residual cache-identity gap).
// Drives the REAL ollama memory embedding adapter `create()` for ONE provider id
// ("ollama") against two different endpoints, then computes the EXACT provider
// cache key memory-core persists — `hashText(JSON.stringify(cacheKeyData))`
// (extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-reindex-state.ts:55). On this branch
// the two endpoints yield different keys (endpoint change -> reindex); on main
// they collide (endpoint change -> silent stale embedding reuse). Run:
//   pnpm test extensions/ollama/src/memory-cache-identity.proof.test.ts
import fs from "node:fs/promises";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import type { OpenClawConfig } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk";
import { hashText } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/memory-core-host-engine-storage";
import { expect, it } from "vitest";
import { ollamaMemoryEmbeddingProviderAdapter as adapter } from "./memory-embedding-adapter.js";

async function providerKeyFor(remote: {
  baseUrl: string;
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
}): Promise<{ cacheKeyData: Record<string, unknown>; providerKey: string }> {
  const created = await adapter.create({
    config: {} as OpenClawConfig,
    provider: "ollama", // SAME provider id for both endpoints
    model: "nomic-embed-text",
    remote,
  } as Parameters<typeof adapter.create>[0]);
  const cacheKeyData = (created.runtime.cacheKeyData ?? {}) as Record<string, unknown>;
  // Exact key memory-core writes as the index provider identity.
  const providerKey = hashText(JSON.stringify(cacheKeyData));
  return { cacheKeyData, providerKey };
}

it("PROOF #97055: same provider id, different endpoints -> distinct memory cache keys", async () => {
  const endpointA = await providerKeyFor({ baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:11434" });
  const endpointB = await providerKeyFor({ baseUrl: "http://10.0.0.5:11434" });
  const secretHeader = await providerKeyFor({
    baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:11434",
    headers: { authorization: "Bearer super-secret-token" },
  });

  const transcript = [
    "======== REAL ollama memory embedding cache identity ========",
    "provider id (both): ollama    model (both): nomic-embed-text",
    "",
    `endpoint A  baseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:11434`,
    `  cacheKeyData = ${JSON.stringify(endpointA.cacheKeyData)}`,
    `  providerKey  = ${endpointA.providerKey}`,
    "",
    `endpoint B  baseUrl=http://10.0.0.5:11434`,
    `  cacheKeyData = ${JSON.stringify(endpointB.cacheKeyData)}`,
    `  providerKey  = ${endpointB.providerKey}`,
    "",
    `endpoint A + secret authorization header`,
    `  cacheKeyData = ${JSON.stringify(secretHeader.cacheKeyData)}`,
    `  providerKey  = ${secretHeader.providerKey}`,
    "",
    `A vs B providerKey differ:            ${endpointA.providerKey !== endpointB.providerKey}`,
    `secret token absent from cache data:  ${!JSON.stringify(secretHeader.cacheKeyData).includes("super-secret-token")}`,
    "=============================================================",
  ].join("\n");
  await fs.writeFile(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "proof-97055-out.txt"), transcript, "utf8");

  // Endpoint change under one provider id must change the persisted key
  // (so memory-core reindexes instead of reusing the other endpoint's cache).
  expect(endpointA.providerKey).not.toBe(endpointB.providerKey);
  // Secret headers are never baked into the cache identity.
  expect(JSON.stringify(secretHeader.cacheKeyData)).not.toContain("super-secret-token");
});
```
</details>

### Notes

The provider-id **routing** fix for #97055 already landed on `main` (fa5f24b021e, b085184d085) and is covered by main's own tests; this PR is the residual **cache-identity** parity, mirroring the lmstudio adapter. `pnpm tsgo:core` and `pnpm tsgo:extensions` are clean.

**Compatibility (maintainer acceptance item):** adding `baseUrl`/`headers` to the Ollama cache identity changes the provider-key hash for existing Ollama memory indexes, so they rebuild once via the normal memory index rebuild flow on upgrade. Intentional and required to distinguish endpoints; memory indexes are rebuildable.

## What Problem This Solves

Closes #80582.

Short-term memory promotion could treat markdown and QMD scaffolding as durable content. That included empty list/checklist/fence/table fragments, placeholder daily-note headings, generic daily/dreaming headings, QMD hunk wrappers, collapsed persisted heading prefixes, and mixed snippets that carried structural text alongside real content.

## Why This Change Was Made

The fix centralizes short-term snippet promotability in the promotion resolver and applies that resolver across recall recording, store normalization, grounded candidates, ranking, relocation/rehydration, and direct promotion application.

Placeholder-only snippets are skipped. Mixed snippets keep real content while dropping structural wrappers such as QMD hunk headers, generic daily/dreaming headings, collapsed heading prefixes, and bullet-prefixed collapsed heading prefixes. Ordinary non-generic bullets remain intact.

The diagnostic skipped recall event now has an additive `unpromotable-short-term-snippet` reason, with the Plugin SDK API baseline hash regenerated for the exported event type change.

## User Impact

Memory promotion should carry actual remembered content instead of daily-note scaffolding or QMD placeholder text. Existing meaningful snippets still promote, and legacy event readers continue to read recorded recall events while diagnostic readers can see skipped recall records.

## Evidence

### Real-behavior proof — actual `MEMORY.md` written to disk

This drives the **real promotion pipeline** end-to-end against a temp workspace — `rankShortTermPromotionCandidates` -> `applyShortTermPromotions` — with real note files on disk and the real SQLite recall store. No mocks; the only inputs are seeded recalls (one markdown placeholder skeleton `## Tagesnotizen\n-`, one meaningful note), both crossing promotion thresholds. The transcript below is the `MEMORY.md` the production code wrote.

**Before — `origin/main` @ `e43bd3d57bc`** (`applied: 2` — the placeholder is promoted into long-term memory):
```
## Promoted From Short-Term Memory (2026-07-12)

<!-- openclaw-memory-promotion:placeholder -->
- ## Tagesnotizen - [score=0.532 signals=4 recalls=4 avg=0.900 source=memory/2026-04-03.md:1-2]
<!-- openclaw-memory-promotion:meaningful -->
- Keep gateway restarts supervised. [score=0.532 signals=4 recalls=4 avg=0.900 source=memory/2026-04-04.md:3-3]
```

**After — this branch @ `144693f8816`** (`applied: 1` — the placeholder is excluded, the real note is kept):
```
## Promoted From Short-Term Memory (2026-07-12)

<!-- openclaw-memory-promotion:meaningful -->
- Keep gateway restarts supervised. [score=0.532 signals=4 recalls=4 avg=0.900 source=memory/2026-04-04.md:3-3]
```

The harness is a vitest spec asserting the real file contains `Keep gateway restarts supervised.` and does **not** contain `Tagesnotizen`; it passes on this branch and fails on main (`AssertionError: expected '# Long-Term Memory…' not to contain 'Tagesnotizen'`).

<details><summary>Reproduce (drop into <code>extensions/memory-core/src/</code> and <code>pnpm test</code> it on either checkout)</summary>

```ts
// Real-behavior proof for issue #80582 / PR #95598.
// Drives the ACTUAL short-term promotion pipeline (rank -> apply) against a real
// temp workspace and prints the real MEMORY.md written to disk. No mocks: the
// only inputs are a seeded recall store and real filesystem writes by the
// production `applyShortTermPromotions` path. Run:
//   pnpm test extensions/memory-core/src/short-term-promotion.proof.test.ts
import fs from "node:fs/promises";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { afterAll, beforeAll, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { configureMemoryCoreDreamingStateForTests } from "./dreaming-state.js";
import {
  applyShortTermPromotions,
  rankShortTermPromotionCandidates,
  testing,
} from "./short-term-promotion.js";

let root: string;
beforeAll(async () => {
  await configureMemoryCoreDreamingStateForTests();
  root = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "proof-80582-"));
});
afterAll(async () => {
  await fs.rm(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
});

it("PROOF #80582: real MEMORY.md excludes placeholder skeletons, keeps meaningful notes", async () => {
  const workspaceDir = path.join(root, "ws");
  await fs.mkdir(path.join(workspaceDir, "memory"), { recursive: true });

  // Real note files on disk backing the recall entries (the promotion path
  // rehydrates each candidate from its source note before writing).
  await fs.writeFile(
    path.join(workspaceDir, "memory", "2026-04-03.md"),
    "## Tagesnotizen\n-\n",
    "utf8",
  );
  await fs.writeFile(
    path.join(workspaceDir, "memory", "2026-04-04.md"),
    "## Entscheidungen:\n-\n- Keep gateway restarts supervised.\n",
    "utf8",
  );

  // Seed the real recall store the way the running agent would after repeated
  // recalls: two entries that BOTH clear promotion thresholds — one is a
  // markdown placeholder skeleton (heading + empty bullet), one is a real note.
  await testing.writeRawRecallStore(workspaceDir, {
    version: 1,
    updatedAt: "2026-04-04T00:00:00.000Z",
    entries: {
      placeholder: {
        key: "placeholder",
        path: "memory/2026-04-03.md",
        startLine: 1,
        endLine: 2,
        source: "memory",
        snippet: "## Tagesnotizen\n-",
        recallCount: 4,
        dailyCount: 0,
        groundedCount: 0,
        totalScore: 3.6,
        maxScore: 0.95,
        firstRecalledAt: "2026-04-03T00:00:00.000Z",
        lastRecalledAt: "2026-04-04T00:00:00.000Z",
        queryHashes: ["a", "b"],
        recallDays: ["2026-04-03", "2026-04-04"],
        conceptTags: ["notes"],
      },
      meaningful: {
        key: "meaningful",
        path: "memory/2026-04-04.md",
        startLine: 3,
        endLine: 3,
        source: "memory",
        snippet: "- Keep gateway restarts supervised.",
        recallCount: 4,
        dailyCount: 0,
        groundedCount: 0,
        totalScore: 3.6,
        maxScore: 0.95,
        firstRecalledAt: "2026-04-03T00:00:00.000Z",
        lastRecalledAt: "2026-04-04T00:00:00.000Z",
        queryHashes: ["a", "b"],
        recallDays: ["2026-04-03", "2026-04-04"],
        conceptTags: ["notes"],
      },
    },
  });

  const ranked = await rankShortTermPromotionCandidates({
    workspaceDir,
    minScore: 0,
    minRecallCount: 0,
    minUniqueQueries: 0,
  });

  const result = await applyShortTermPromotions({
    workspaceDir,
    candidates: ranked,
    minScore: 0,
    minRecallCount: 0,
    minUniqueQueries: 0,
  });

  const memory = await fs.readFile(result.memoryPath, "utf8").catch(() => "<no MEMORY.md written>");

  const transcript = [
    "================ REAL MEMORY.md ON DISK ================",
    `path: ${path.relative(workspaceDir, result.memoryPath)}`,
    `promotion candidates ranked: ${ranked.map((c) => JSON.stringify(c.snippet)).join(", ") || "(none)"}`,
    `applied: ${result.applied}`,
    "-------------------------------------------------------",
    memory.trimEnd(),
    "=======================================================",
  ].join("\n");
  await fs.writeFile(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "proof-80582-out.txt"), transcript, "utf8");

  // The real file must contain the meaningful note and must NOT contain the
  // placeholder skeleton heading.
  expect(memory).toContain("Keep gateway restarts supervised.");
  expect(memory).not.toContain("Tagesnotizen");
});
```
</details>

### Source-level red-green

`CI=true pnpm test extensions/memory-core/src/short-term-promotion.test.ts` — 99 pass on branch; copying that same test file onto `origin/main` fails 3 (`… to have a length of 1 but got 2`), because main records the placeholder skeletons this branch filters. `pnpm tsgo:core`, `pnpm tsgo:extensions`, and the plugin-sdk surface budgets are clean.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where remote memory provider calls would reject successful JSON responses when the provider returned a valid decimal `Content-Length` header with leading zeros, such as `00017`.

## Why This Change Was Made

The memory-host SDK bounded JSON response reader now accepts digit-only `Content-Length` values while still rejecting empty, non-decimal, and unsafe integer values. This keeps the fix inside the response-size guard used by `postJson` and batch file upload responses without changing request routing, auth, provider selection, or response body limits.

## User Impact

Remote memory and embedding workflows can parse successful JSON responses from providers that emit leading-zero content lengths, while malformed or oversized responses remain blocked at the same guard boundary.

## Evidence

- Claim proved: `postJson` and `uploadBatchJsonlFile` accept successful JSON responses with leading-zero `Content-Length` headers from a real HTTP response while preserving bounded response parsing.
- Current-head proof at `91b4b4062cbf3f0b85bdc6049a51a604ba5ba0f0`: a repo-local runtime transcript started a localhost HTTP server, returned actual HTTP responses with `Content-Length: 0002` and `Content-Length: 00017`, and called the production SDK entrypoints with Node fetch.

```json
{
  "proof": "real localhost HTTP response with leading-zero Content-Length",
  "postJson": {
    "contentLength": "0002",
    "result": {}
  },
  "uploadBatchJsonlFile": {
    "contentLength": "00017",
    "result": "file_123"
  },
  "serverRequests": [
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/json",
      "bytes": 13
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/files",
      "bytes": 365
    }
  ]
}
```

- Focused regression proof: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs packages/memory-host-sdk/src/host/post-json.test.ts packages/memory-host-sdk/src/host/batch-upload.test.ts` passed with 2 shards and 15 tests.
- Diff hygiene: `git diff --check upstream/main...HEAD` passed.
- Review proof: `python .agents\skills\autoreview\scripts\autoreview --mode branch --base upstream/main` passed with no accepted/actionable findings.

